# UPGRADE-09

# Production Deployment Stack, Reliability Engineering,

# Identity, Secrets, Observability, Backup/PITR,

# Rolling Upgrades & Disaster-Recovery Verification

You are working directly inside ZCoder after Upgrade-08.

Upgrade-08 reportedly provides:

* SQLite local mode
* PostgreSQL control-plane architecture
* migrations
* atomic claims
* fencing tokens
* leases
* durable outbox
* webhook inbox deduplication
* GitHub App fleet registry
* installation routing
* repository trust policies
* tenant isolation
* Operations API
* 544 tests passing
* 2 optional skips
* FINAL PASS

Treat every claim as untrusted until verified.

============================================================
MISSION
=======

Turn ZCoder into a deployable and operable production service with
measurable reliability properties.

This release is NOT primarily about adding more agent features.

The objective is:

CODE
↓
BUILD
↓
SIGNED / SCANNED ARTIFACT
↓
DEPLOY
↓
HEALTH / READINESS
↓
OBSERVE
↓
SCALE
↓
ROLL
↓
BACK UP
↓
RESTORE
↓
FAIL OVER / RECOVER
↓
PROVE THE SYSTEM STILL WORKS

Target deployment architecture:

```
                Internet / Internal Network
                          │
                          ▼
                 Ingress / Load Balancer
                          │
             ┌────────────┴─────────────┐
             ▼                          ▼
      Control Plane API A        Control Plane API B
             │                          │
             └────────────┬─────────────┘
                          │
                          ▼
                     PostgreSQL
                   Primary / HA
                          │
             ┌────────────┼────────────┐
             ▼            ▼            ▼
          Worker A     Worker B     Worker C
             │            │            │
             ├ Agent SDK  ├ Managed    ├ Direct
             │            │ Agents     │
             └────────────┴────────────┘

                          │
                   OpenTelemetry
                          │
             ┌────────────┼────────────┐
             ▼            ▼            ▼
          Metrics       Traces       Logs
             │
          Alerts
```

Keep SQLite/local mode working.

Production mode must use explicit production configuration and must not
silently degrade to unsafe local behavior.

============================================================
PHASE 0 — PROVE UPGRADE-08 FIRST
================================

Before implementing deployment infrastructure, verify the control plane.

Run:

git status --short
git log -30 --oneline
git tag --sort=-version:refname | head -20

python -m pytest -q
python main.py --version
python main.py --help
python -m build
git diff --check

Inspect:

control_plane.py
agent_runtime.py
github_orchestrator.py
database/storage modules
migration code
tests/test_control_plane.py
release gate
CI workflows
deployment files if any

Create:

UPGRADE-08 PRODUCTION PROOF MATRIX

Capability
Previous Claim
Unit Evidence
Real PostgreSQL Evidence
Multi-Process Evidence
Failure-Recovery Evidence
Verdict

Allowed verdicts:

VERIFIED
UNIT_ONLY
MOCK_ONLY
SINGLE_PROCESS_ONLY
POSTGRES_NOT_PROVEN
PARTIAL
INCORRECT
MISSING

Do not proceed to production claims while any P0 item is UNIT_ONLY or
POSTGRES_NOT_PROVEN.

============================================================

1. REAL POSTGRESQL INTEGRATION TEST
   ============================================================

Bring up an actual PostgreSQL instance using:

* CI service container
* Docker Compose
* Testcontainers
* or equivalent isolated test infrastructure

Do not use SQLite compatibility tests as evidence of PostgreSQL
correctness.

Test actual:

transactions
row locking
SKIP LOCKED behavior where used
unique constraints
fencing
lease renewal
outbox claims
inbox deduplication
migration execution
rollback behavior

============================================================
2. MULTI-PROCESS CLAIM TEST
===========================

Start:

1 PostgreSQL
2 independent worker OS processes

Queue many jobs.

Prove:

each job claimed once
no duplicate execution
fencing generation is monotonic
leases renew
worker identity persists
events remain ordered per job

Do not use two Python objects inside one process as proof.

============================================================
3. WORKER CRASH PROOF
=====================

Worker A:

claim job
begin work

Kill Worker A abruptly.

Wait for controlled lease expiration.

Worker B must:

detect expiration
reconcile external state
acquire a newer fencing generation
continue or correctly stop

A stale Worker A write must be rejected if it returns.

Test this explicitly.

============================================================
4. CONTROL PLANE CRASH PROOF
============================

Run workers independently.

Kill API/scheduler.

Workers should continue according to architecture.

Restart API/scheduler.

Verify:

jobs
workers
leases
events
approvals
GitHub mappings
outbox

are reconstructed from durable state.

============================================================
5. DATABASE RESTART PROOF
=========================

Restart PostgreSQL while the system is active.

Verify:

connection pool recovery
transaction rollback
lease behavior
outbox safety
no duplicate external mutation

Do not claim DB restart tolerance if the service requires manual process
restart unless documented.

============================================================
6. UPGRADE-08 GATE CORRECTION
=============================

If Upgrade-08 marked:

MULTI_HOST_CLAIMS: PASS

without a real shared PostgreSQL multi-process test, correct the audit
history.

Do not silently preserve a false PASS.

Record:

PREVIOUS_FALSE_POSITIVE

where warranted.

============================================================
7. DEPLOYMENT PROFILES
======================

Provide at least:

local
development
production

Example conceptual behavior:

local:
SQLite
one process
no external ingress requirement

development:
Compose
PostgreSQL
API
worker

production:
PostgreSQL
authenticated API
secure secrets
multiple replicas
health checks
observability
backup requirements

Do not infer production merely from DEBUG=false.

============================================================
8. DOCKERFILE
=============

Create/harden production Docker images.

Requirements:

multi-stage build where beneficial
minimal runtime dependencies
non-root user
deterministic package install
no compiler toolchain in runtime image unless needed
explicit entrypoint
health behavior
clean signal propagation

Do not copy:

.git
.env
tests secrets
local databases
credentials
dist junk

Use .dockerignore.

============================================================
9. IMAGE USER
=============

Application container must not run as root by default.

Use a dedicated UID/GID.

Ensure writable paths are explicit.

Do not chmod 777 as a workaround.

============================================================
10. IMAGE IMMUTABILITY
======================

Treat application container filesystem as read-only where practical.

Writable state belongs in:

temporary directory
workspace volume
explicit persistent store

Control-plane state belongs in PostgreSQL, not local container disk.

============================================================
11. CONTAINER SIGNAL HANDLING
=============================

PID 1 must handle SIGTERM correctly.

Test:

API shutdown
worker shutdown

Worker should:

stop taking new jobs
checkpoint/release safely
close DB connections

Do not wait for lease expiry unnecessarily during graceful shutdown if
safe release is possible.

============================================================
12. DOCKER COMPOSE
==================

Provide production-like developer stack:

services:

postgres
api
worker
optional observability components

Use health dependencies correctly.

Do not treat Compose as production HA.

Document:

docker compose up

for reproducible local integration.

============================================================
13. COMPOSE POSTGRES
====================

Do not expose PostgreSQL publicly by default.

Use internal network.

Persist database data in explicit volume.

Use healthcheck.

Do not embed production passwords into compose.yaml.

============================================================
14. KUBERNETES BASE MANIFESTS
=============================

Provide production-capable Kubernetes resources or Helm templates.

At minimum:

API Deployment
Worker Deployment
Service
ConfigMap where appropriate
Secret references
ServiceAccount
PodDisruptionBudget
NetworkPolicy where supported
HorizontalPodAutoscaler where justified

Do not deploy PostgreSQL inside the same chart as a fake HA production
database unless explicitly marked development-only.

============================================================
15. HELM CHART
==============

Prefer a maintainable Helm chart if project conventions allow.

Suggested:

deploy/helm/zcoder/

Chart.yaml
values.yaml
templates/

Support values for:

image
replicas
resources
database
worker concurrency
ingress
service
telemetry
auth
secrets
GitHub App
Anthropic config

Validate:

helm lint

and render manifests in CI.

============================================================
16. KUBERNETES READINESS
========================

Readiness must answer:

"Can this instance safely receive traffic?"

API readiness may depend on:

database reachable
migrations compatible
required configuration valid

Do not make GitHub/Anthropic transient outages necessarily fail
readiness unless architecture requires them for all requests.

============================================================
17. KUBERNETES LIVENESS
=======================

Liveness answers:

"Is the process internally healthy enough that restart is useful?"

Do not make liveness depend on every external dependency.

Otherwise a PostgreSQL outage can cause an unnecessary pod restart
storm.

============================================================
18. STARTUP PROBE
=================

If startup may take time because of:

initialization
migration checks
cache loading

use startup semantics rather than huge liveness delays.

============================================================
19. HEALTH ENDPOINTS
====================

Implement clearly:

/health/live
/health/ready

Optionally:

/health/details

Detailed health output must require appropriate access if it exposes
sensitive operational information.

============================================================
20. MIGRATION SAFETY ON KUBERNETES
==================================

Do not let every API replica independently run unsafe schema migration.

Implement one controlled strategy:

migration Job
init process with DB lock
release pipeline migration step

Document it.

============================================================
21. ROLLING DEPLOYMENT
======================

Configure rolling updates so old and new replicas overlap safely.

Prove:

old worker and new worker coexist
protocol/version compatibility works
fencing remains safe
no duplicate work
API continues serving

============================================================
22. ROLLING DB MIGRATION
========================

Apply expand/migrate/contract where schema changes require compatibility
between versions.

Test at least one representative migration with:

old worker
new schema
new worker

Do not claim zero-downtime migration for schemas not designed for it.

============================================================
23. POD DISRUPTION BUDGET
=========================

Protect API availability against voluntary disruptions where multiple
replicas exist.

Do not configure impossible PDB values such as requiring all replicas
while also expecting routine node maintenance.

============================================================
24. RESOURCE REQUESTS
=====================

Set realistic baseline:

CPU request
memory request

Workers and API should have separate profiles.

Do not copy arbitrary internet values.

Measure or document assumptions.

============================================================
25. RESOURCE LIMITS
===================

Decide deliberately whether CPU limits are appropriate.

Set memory limits where useful.

Test behavior near limits.

Do not configure tiny memory limits simply to make manifests look
complete.

============================================================
26. HORIZONTAL AUTOSCALING
==========================

API scaling signals may include:

CPU
request rate
latency

Worker scaling should preferably reflect:

queue depth
job wait time
worker utilization

CPU-only scaling may be a poor proxy for agent job demand.

Design metric abstraction.

============================================================
27. SCALE-TO-ZERO
=================

Do not scale workers to zero if:

lease reconciliation
webhook processing
required background maintenance

depends on a worker existing.

Separate maintenance roles if needed.

============================================================
28. NETWORK POLICY
==================

Define intended flows:

Ingress -> API
API -> PostgreSQL
workers -> PostgreSQL
API/workers -> GitHub
workers -> Anthropic
telemetry -> Collector

Default deny where cluster/network implementation supports it.

Do not permit workers arbitrary east-west traffic unnecessarily.

============================================================
29. EGRESS POLICY
=================

Worker egress is security-sensitive.

Represent:

GitHub API
Anthropic API
approved MCP targets
package registries if explicitly required

Do not pretend Kubernetes NetworkPolicy alone provides DNS/FQDN-aware
security on every implementation.

Document limitations.

============================================================
30. SERVICE ACCOUNTS
====================

Use dedicated Kubernetes ServiceAccounts.

Do not mount broad Kubernetes API credentials into pods that do not need
them.

Disable automatic service account token mounting where unnecessary.

============================================================
31. KUBERNETES RBAC
===================

If ZCoder does not need Kubernetes API access:

grant none.

If deployment/operations integrations need it later:

create minimum role.

Never use cluster-admin by default.

============================================================
32. POD SECURITY
================

Harden pod/container securityContext.

Consider:

runAsNonRoot
allowPrivilegeEscalation=false
readOnlyRootFilesystem
capabilities drop
seccomp profile

Account for worker sandbox requirements explicitly.

Do not weaken the API pod because one specialized worker needs extra
capability.

============================================================
33. WORKER POOLS
================

Different worker security requirements may justify pools:

standard
sandbox
trusted
high-isolation

Scheduler uses worker capabilities.

Do not let a restricted repository run on a privileged worker merely
because it is idle.

============================================================
34. NODE PLACEMENT
==================

Support optional:

nodeSelector
affinity
tolerations

for specialized workers.

Do not hardcode provider-specific labels.

============================================================
35. SECRET MANAGEMENT ABSTRACTION
=================================

Extend SecretRef.

Potential providers:

environment
mounted file
Kubernetes Secret reference
external secret manager adapter

Do not hardcode one cloud secret manager unless requested.

============================================================
36. NO PLAINTEXT SECRETS IN VALUES
==================================

Helm values should reference existing secret names/keys.

Do not encourage:

githubPrivateKey: |
-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----

in committed values.

============================================================
37. SECRET ROTATION
===================

Document behavior for rotating:

GitHub App private key
webhook secret
database credentials
Anthropic credentials

Prefer reload/restart semantics that avoid downtime where practical.

============================================================
38. GITHUB ACTIONS OIDC
=======================

For cloud deployments initiated from GitHub Actions:

prefer OIDC-based short-lived cloud identity where supported.

Do not require static long-lived cloud access keys in GitHub Secrets
when OIDC can replace them.

Scope trust conditions narrowly.

============================================================
39. DEPLOYMENT ENVIRONMENTS
===========================

Use GitHub Environments for production deployment workflows where
appropriate.

Apply:

environment protection
branch/tag restrictions
required approval where required by policy

Do not let an arbitrary PR deploy to production.

============================================================
40. RELEASE ARTIFACT
====================

Build once.

Promote the same immutable image/artifact between:

staging
production

Do not rebuild from source separately for production if avoidable.

============================================================
41. IMAGE TAGGING
=================

Publish immutable identity such as:

version
Git SHA

Avoid production deployment solely by mutable:

latest

If convenience tags exist, deployment should still resolve immutable
digest/tag.

============================================================
42. CONTAINER IMAGE DIGEST
==========================

Record image digest in release report.

Support deployment by digest where practical.

============================================================
43. SBOM
========

Generate release SBOM.

CycloneDX or SPDX is acceptable.

Cover at least container/application dependencies.

============================================================
44. PROVENANCE
==============

Where build infrastructure supports it, emit artifact provenance.

Do not falsely claim SLSA compliance unless requirements are actually
met.

============================================================
45. VULNERABILITY SCAN
======================

Scan:

Python dependencies
container image

Define severity policy.

Do not fail every release forever on an irrelevant unfixed transitive
finding without triage policy.

But known exploitable critical vulnerabilities must block production.

============================================================
46. SIGNING
===========

Support artifact/container signing where practical.

Keep signing keys out of repository.

Keyless/OIDC-backed signing is welcome if environment supports it.

Do not make unconfigured signing silently PASS.

============================================================
47. OBSERVABILITY ARCHITECTURE
==============================

Adopt OpenTelemetry as telemetry abstraction if consistent with current
code.

Instrument:

API
scheduler
workers
database
GitHub calls
Anthropic calls
job execution
outbox
reconciler

Avoid vendor lock-in in application instrumentation.

============================================================
48. TELEMETRY SIGNALS
=====================

Support:

metrics
traces
logs correlation

Do not force all logs through OTLP if existing structured logging is
better retained.

Correlate using:

trace_id
job_id
attempt_id
worker_id

Do not expose these as high-cardinality Prometheus labels
indiscriminately.

============================================================
49. OTEL COLLECTOR
==================

Provide optional OpenTelemetry Collector deployment/configuration.

Do not send application telemetry directly to many vendors if a
Collector architecture is selected.

Support exporter configuration.

============================================================
50. TELEMETRY SECURITY
======================

Collector endpoints must not be anonymously exposed to the internet.

Configure:

authentication/encryption where required
bounded receivers
memory protection
batching
sensitive-data scrubbing

Never export:

API keys
Authorization
private keys
raw vault secret values

============================================================
51. PROMETHEUS METRICS
======================

Expose useful application metrics.

Examples:

zcoder_jobs_queued
zcoder_jobs_running
zcoder_job_wait_seconds
zcoder_job_duration_seconds
zcoder_worker_active
zcoder_worker_lease_expirations_total
zcoder_outbox_pending
zcoder_outbox_failures_total
zcoder_webhooks_total
zcoder_github_api_errors_total
zcoder_anthropic_errors_total
zcoder_db_pool_in_use
zcoder_approvals_pending

Use bounded labels.

============================================================
52. RED METRICS
===============

For API:

rate
errors
duration

For workers:

queue wait
execution duration
failure rate
capacity utilization

For outbox:

backlog
age
delivery latency
failure rate

============================================================
53. DATABASE METRICS
====================

Monitor at least:

connectivity
pool saturation
transaction errors
lock wait symptoms
query latency
storage/connection pressure

Do not collect sensitive query parameters.

============================================================
54. ALERTING
============

Create actionable alerts.

Examples:

APIUnavailable
WorkerFleetEmpty
QueueBacklogGrowing
OldestQueuedJobTooOld
OutboxStuck
WebhookFailureRateHigh
DatabaseUnavailable
LeaseExpirySpike
GitHubRateLimitCritical
AnthropicFailureRateHigh
BackupMissing
RestoreDrillStale

Every alert should include:

meaning
impact
first diagnostic steps
runbook link

============================================================
55. ALERTMANAGER
================

If shipping Prometheus stack examples, configure Alertmanager or leave
notification routing explicitly external.

Do not assume Prometheus itself delivers production notifications.

============================================================
56. DASHBOARDS
==============

Provide dashboard definitions or documented panels.

At minimum:

Platform overview
Jobs
Workers
PostgreSQL
GitHub
Anthropic
Outbox/webhooks
Cost

Dashboards should answer operational questions, not merely look good.

============================================================
57. SLOs
========

Define initial service objectives.

Possible dimensions:

API availability
job start latency
control-plane event durability
webhook processing latency
outbox delivery latency

Do not invent unrealistic 99.999% goals.

Derive initial targets from expected deployment scale.

============================================================
58. SLI DEFINITIONS
===================

Document exact numerator/denominator.

Example:

API availability =
successful eligible API requests /
all eligible API requests

Exclude only explicitly defined categories.

============================================================
59. ERROR BUDGET
================

If SLO framework is implemented, calculate error budget.

Use it as operational signal, not marketing text.

============================================================
60. TRACING
===========

Trace important flow:

GitHub webhook
→ event
→ scheduler
→ job
→ worker
→ runtime
→ GitHub PR
→ CI
→ repair

Preserve correlation across async outbox boundaries.

============================================================
61. LOG RETENTION
=================

Define configurable retention.

Do not keep:

agent logs
GitHub logs
events

forever merely because storage is cheap today.

Audit/event retention may differ from debug logs.

============================================================
62. PII / SENSITIVE LOG REVIEW
==============================

Audit whether repository names, prompts, code snippets or usernames are
sensitive in a deployment context.

Provide redaction/minimization controls.

============================================================
63. POSTGRESQL BACKUP STRATEGY
==============================

Implement/document at least one tested production backup strategy.

For simple logical backup:

pg_dump / pg_restore

For production recovery requirements consider:

base backup
WAL archiving
PITR

Do not call pg_dump alone a complete low-RPO disaster-recovery strategy.

============================================================
64. PITR
========

Where production profile claims PITR:

configure/test:

base backup
WAL archive
recovery target

Do not claim PITR merely because archive_mode appears in a sample config.

Perform restore.

============================================================
65. BACKUP ENCRYPTION
=====================

Production backups may contain:

jobs
repository metadata
events
audit history

Protect them appropriately.

Do not store backup encryption keys beside the backups.

============================================================
66. BACKUP RETENTION
====================

Define policy:

daily
weekly
WAL retention
or deployment-specific equivalents

Document tradeoffs.

Do not hardcode one policy as universal.

============================================================
67. RESTORE TEST
================

Mandatory.

Create data:

job
events
repository mapping
approval
outbox result

Back it up.

Restore into fresh PostgreSQL.

Verify identities and state.

A backup that has never been restored is not considered verified.

============================================================
68. RPO / RTO
=============

Document:

target RPO
target RTO

for each deployment profile.

Then measure restore drill performance separately.

Do not guarantee numbers without evidence.

============================================================
69. DISASTER RECOVERY RUNBOOK
=============================

Create:

docs/DISASTER-RECOVERY.md

Cover:

database lost
control plane lost
all workers lost
GitHub App key compromised
Anthropic credential compromised
webhook secret compromised
bad deployment
schema migration failure

============================================================
70. BAD DEPLOYMENT ROLLBACK
===========================

Test application rollback:

new version unhealthy
→ readiness fails
→ no traffic
→ rollback

Ensure DB migration compatibility.

Code rollback is not safe if migration already destroyed old-schema
compatibility.

============================================================
71. MIGRATION ROLLBACK POLICY
=============================

Do not promise automatic database rollback for every migration.

Prefer forward-fix and reversible/compatible migration design.

Classify migration:

online-compatible
maintenance-required
irreversible

============================================================
72. DRILL — WORKER LOSS
=======================

Simulate losing a worker node/pod.

Verify:

lease expires
job reconciles
new worker claims
stale fencing token blocked

============================================================
73. DRILL — API INSTANCE LOSS
=============================

Kill one API replica.

Traffic should continue through another healthy replica when multiple
replicas are configured.

============================================================
74. DRILL — DATABASE CONNECTION LOSS
====================================

Temporarily block PostgreSQL connectivity.

Observe:

readiness
retry behavior
metrics
alerts
recovery

Avoid restart storms.

============================================================
75. DRILL — GITHUB OUTAGE
=========================

Simulate GitHub API failure.

Expected:

outbox/retry
backoff
rate limiting
no duplicate PR/merge
visibility to operator

============================================================
76. DRILL — ANTHROPIC OUTAGE
============================

Simulate Anthropic transient failure.

Expected:

bounded retries
provider state visible
new work may pause according to policy
existing state remains durable

Do not endlessly retry expensive calls.

============================================================
77. DRILL — STUCK OUTBOX
========================

Create an external action that repeatedly fails.

Verify:

retry
backoff
DEAD state
alert
operator retry path

============================================================
78. CHAOS TEST BOUNDARY
=======================

Do not introduce a massive chaos platform merely to satisfy this prompt.

Use deterministic failure injection first.

Containers/process killing and network interruption are sufficient for
Upgrade-09.

============================================================
79. AUTHENTICATION FOR OPERATIONS UI
====================================

Production Web/API must require authentication.

Implement a clean auth boundary.

Support at least one production-capable method appropriate to the
project.

Potential architecture:

OIDC identity provider
→ validated identity
→ ZCoder role

Do not build a custom password database unless there is a strong reason.

============================================================
80. OIDC VALIDATION
===================

If OIDC is implemented:

validate:

issuer
audience
signature
expiry
nonce/state where applicable

Do not decode JWT and trust claims without verification.

============================================================
81. ROLE MAPPING
================

Map authenticated principals to:

VIEWER
OPERATOR
ADMIN

Enforce authorization server-side.

Do not rely on hidden UI buttons.

============================================================
82. SESSION SECURITY
====================

If browser sessions are used:

secure cookies
HttpOnly
SameSite
CSRF protection where required
session expiration

Do not put long-lived identity tokens in localStorage if avoidable.

============================================================
83. BREAK-GLASS ADMIN
=====================

If emergency admin access exists:

make it explicit
audited
disabled by default where possible

Do not create universal hardcoded credentials.

============================================================
84. AUDIT AUTH EVENTS
=====================

Audit:

login
logout
denied privileged action
role change
policy change
secret reference change

Do not log raw identity tokens.

============================================================
85. TLS
=======

Production ingress should use TLS.

Support termination at:

Ingress
load balancer
reverse proxy

Application should correctly understand forwarded scheme/client context
only from trusted proxies.

============================================================
86. TRUSTED PROXY
=================

Do not trust arbitrary:

X-Forwarded-For
X-Forwarded-Proto

unless request originated from configured proxy boundary.

============================================================
87. SECURITY HEADERS
====================

For web console configure suitable:

Content-Security-Policy
frame restrictions
content-type protections
referrer policy

Do not blindly paste a policy that breaks the application.

============================================================
88. API RATE LIMITING
=====================

Protect externally reachable endpoints.

Particularly:

login
webhooks
expensive job submission

Do not rate-limit internal worker heartbeat through a generic tiny
public API quota.

============================================================
89. PAYLOAD LIMITS
==================

Set explicit request limits for:

webhooks
job task input
uploaded artifacts
API JSON

Protect against memory exhaustion.

============================================================
90. IMAGE/DEPENDENCY UPDATE POLICY
==================================

Pin base images intentionally.

Use automated dependency/update tooling only through review.

Do not auto-deploy base-image updates to production without tests.

============================================================
91. KUBERNETES CONFIG VALIDATION
================================

CI must run appropriate validation:

helm lint
helm template

plus schema/static checks available in the environment.

Do not require access to a production cluster for normal PR validation.

============================================================
92. EPHEMERAL DEPLOYMENT TEST
=============================

Use:

kind
k3d
minikube
or equivalent

if practical.

Deploy:

API
worker
PostgreSQL test instance

Then smoke test:

health
job submission
worker claim
event persistence

This is stronger evidence than manifest rendering alone.

============================================================
93. UPGRADE TEST
================

In ephemeral environment:

deploy previous version
create active durable state
deploy new version using rolling strategy
verify state/job continuity

This is mandatory before claiming rolling-upgrade safety.

============================================================
94. HPA TEST
============

Do not require real cloud autoscaling.

Validate HPA manifests and, where feasible, synthetic scaling behavior.

State clearly what was not live-proven.

============================================================
95. LOAD TEST
=============

Add a bounded synthetic load scenario.

Measure:

API throughput
queueing
claim latency
DB connections
job scheduling overhead
webhook ingestion

Do not spend Anthropic tokens for load testing.

Use FakeRuntime.

============================================================
96. CAPACITY BASELINE
=====================

Report measured environment and results.

Example:

API replicas:
workers:
PostgreSQL:
jobs:
webhook rate:

p50:
p95:
p99:

Do not publish performance claims without hardware/environment context.

============================================================
97. QUEUE BACKPRESSURE TEST
===========================

Submit more jobs than worker capacity.

Verify:

queue grows safely
API remains responsive
DB does not thrash
scheduler remains bounded
job priority/fairness stays correct

============================================================
98. DATABASE POOL SATURATION
============================

Artificially constrain pool.

Verify:

bounded waiting
timeouts
useful metrics
no deadlock cascade

============================================================
99. COST OBSERVABILITY
======================

Expose aggregated costs by:

job
repository
installation
runtime

Avoid high-cardinality Prometheus cost labels.

Detailed cost belongs in DB/API/dashboard.

============================================================
100. DEPLOYMENT COST AWARENESS
==============================

Document major infrastructure cost drivers:

workers
PostgreSQL
observability retention
egress
Anthropic usage

Do not invent a universal monthly cost without deployment assumptions.

============================================================
101. PRODUCTION CONFIG SCHEMA
=============================

Formalize configuration.

Validate:

database
auth
GitHub App
Anthropic
worker
telemetry
backup
security

Unknown/deprecated critical fields should produce warnings/errors.

============================================================
102. CONFIG PRECEDENCE
======================

Document:

defaults
config file
environment
secret provider
CLI

Avoid ambiguous precedence.

============================================================
103. CONFIG DUMP
================

Provide:

zcoder config show-effective

Redact secrets.

Useful for operations/debugging.

============================================================
104. DOCTOR
===========

Expand:

zcoder doctor

Check:

database
migration
GitHub App
Anthropic
workers
telemetry
backup freshness
build/version
configuration security

Support:

--json

============================================================
105. DEPLOYMENT CLI
===================

Do not reinvent kubectl/helm.

ZCoder may provide:

zcoder deploy check

but actual deployment should remain standard infrastructure tooling.

============================================================
106. RELEASE WORKFLOW
=====================

Design pipeline:

test
→ integration
→ build
→ SBOM
→ scan
→ sign
→ publish immutable artifact
→ staging
→ smoke
→ approval
→ production
→ post-deploy verify

Every stage must consume the same release identity.

============================================================
107. GITHUB ENVIRONMENT PROTECTION
==================================

Production deployment job must use explicit deployment environment where
GitHub Actions is the chosen CI/CD system.

Do not expose production credentials to pre-approval jobs.

============================================================
108. OIDC CLOUD AUTH
====================

If deployment target is AWS/Azure/GCP or another OIDC-capable provider,
configure GitHub OIDC appropriately.

Do not assume a provider if none is selected.

Provide provider-neutral architecture and one example only if justified.

============================================================
109. DEPLOYMENT APPROVAL
========================

Autonomous coding success is NOT equivalent to production deployment
approval.

Production mutation remains separately authorized.

Do not let ZCoder approve its own production deployment by default.

============================================================
110. POST-DEPLOY VALIDATION
===========================

After deployment:

check rollout
readiness
API smoke
database schema
worker registration
queue health
GitHub webhook processing
telemetry

If critical validation fails:

halt promotion / rollback according to policy.

============================================================
111. RUNBOOKS
=============

Create useful runbooks:

docs/runbooks/API-DOWN.md
docs/runbooks/DB-DOWN.md
docs/runbooks/QUEUE-BACKLOG.md
docs/runbooks/WORKER-LOSS.md
docs/runbooks/GITHUB-OUTAGE.md
docs/runbooks/ANTHROPIC-OUTAGE.md
docs/runbooks/OUTBOX-STUCK.md
docs/runbooks/BACKUP-RESTORE.md

No empty templates.

============================================================
112. SECURITY THREAT MODEL UPDATE
=================================

Expand threat model for:

Kubernetes
Ingress
OIDC
CI/CD
container supply chain
telemetry pipeline
backup storage
secret manager
compromised worker
malicious container image
stolen signing identity

============================================================
113. SECURITY TESTS
===================

Add tests for:

anonymous mutation denied
viewer mutation denied
OIDC invalid issuer
OIDC invalid audience
expired token
secret redaction
webhook payload size
untrusted proxy header
sensitive endpoint access
config output redaction

============================================================
114. DEPLOYMENT SECURITY TEST
=============================

Inspect rendered Kubernetes manifests.

Fail CI if production profile unintentionally has:

privileged=true
runAsUser=0
hostNetwork=true
hostPID=true
wildcard RBAC
plaintext known secret values

Allow explicitly reviewed exceptions only.

============================================================
115. BACKUP MONITOR
===================

Production profile should expose backup freshness state.

A successful backup command is not enough.

Track:

last backup
last verified restore
WAL/archive status where applicable

============================================================
116. RESTORE DRILL AGE
======================

Alert if restore verification has not occurred within configured policy.

Do not infer restorability solely from backup timestamps.

============================================================
117. DEPLOYMENT HISTORY
=======================

Track:

version
image digest
deployment time
actor
environment
migration version
result

Do not put this only in CI logs.

============================================================
118. ROLLBACK HISTORY
=====================

Record rollback:

from
to
reason
actor
result

Audit production mutation.

============================================================
119. MAINTENANCE MODE
=====================

Support controlled maintenance behavior if schema/operations require it.

Do not use maintenance mode for every deploy.

============================================================
120. GRACEFUL DRAIN
===================

Worker deployment termination should:

mark draining
stop claims
finish/checkpoint work
release safely
terminate

Kubernetes termination grace period must cover the intended shutdown
behavior.

============================================================
121. LONG-RUNNING JOBS DURING DEPLOYMENT
========================================

Define policy:

finish on old worker
checkpoint/reconcile
remote managed job reconnect

Do not kill long-running jobs simply to roll out a patch unless policy
allows it.

============================================================
122. PROTOCOL COMPATIBILITY
===========================

Persist protocol/version compatibility where API/workers communicate.

New control plane must not assign unsupported task formats to older
workers during rollout.

============================================================
123. FEATURE GATES
==================

For risky new infrastructure behavior, use explicit feature/config gates
when useful.

Do not accumulate permanent dead flags.

============================================================
124. PRODUCTION RELEASE GATE
============================

Extend release gate substantially.

Required:

SOURCE_TRUTH
TESTS
BUILD
INSTALL

POSTGRES_REAL
MULTI_PROCESS
WORKER_CRASH_RECOVERY
DB_RESTART_RECOVERY

CONTAINER
COMPOSE
KUBERNETES
HELM
ROLLING_UPGRADE

AUTH
SECRETS
NETWORK_POLICY
SUPPLY_CHAIN

OBSERVABILITY
ALERTING
SLO

BACKUP
RESTORE
PITR

SECURITY
DOCS

FINAL

A unit-test-only success cannot satisfy:

POSTGRES_REAL
MULTI_PROCESS
RESTORE
ROLLING_UPGRADE

============================================================
125. RELEASE-GATE EVIDENCE
==========================

Each gate should record:

status
evidence command/test
timestamp
environment
notes

Example:

POSTGRES_REAL:
PASS
evidence:
pytest tests/integration/postgres -q

Do not let code set all booleans to True without evidence.

============================================================
126. CI JOBS
============

Suggested CI:

lint
typecheck
unit
full-dependencies
postgres-integration
multi-worker
package-build
wheel-install
container-build
container-scan
helm-lint
k8s-render
ephemeral-k8s
security
conformance
release-gate

Heavy ephemeral deployment tests may run on main/release/nightly if PR
cost is excessive.

============================================================
127. NIGHTLY RELIABILITY TEST
=============================

Create optional scheduled workflow for:

PostgreSQL restart
worker crash
backup/restore
ephemeral Kubernetes deployment

Do not run destructive tests against production infrastructure.

============================================================
128. VERSIONING
===============

Inspect actual release/tag history.

This upgrade represents production deployment/reliability functionality.

Choose SemVer based on actual published history.

Do not continue expanding an unreleased historical version forever if
the project now contains multiple major capability increments.

Record why the version was chosen.

============================================================
129. REQUIRED DOCUMENTS
=======================

Update:

README.md
ARCHITECTURE.md
ROADMAP.md
CHANGELOG.md
SECURITY.md
CONTRIBUTING.md

Add:

docs/UPGRADE-09.md
docs/DEPLOYMENT.md
docs/KUBERNETES.md
docs/HELM.md
docs/OBSERVABILITY.md
docs/SLO.md
docs/BACKUP-RESTORE.md
docs/DISASTER-RECOVERY.md
docs/IDENTITY.md
docs/SECRETS.md
docs/RELEASE-PROCESS.md

and substantive runbooks.

============================================================
130. FINAL VERIFICATION COMMANDS
================================

At minimum:

python -m pytest -q
python main.py --help
python main.py --version
python -m build
git diff --check
git status --short

Then separately:

PostgreSQL integration suite
multi-process worker suite
worker crash test
database restart test
Compose smoke
container smoke
helm lint
helm template
ephemeral Kubernetes test
rolling-upgrade test
backup/restore test
release gate

Run available:

ruff check .
black --check .
mypy .

Do not omit failed commands.

============================================================
131. DEFINITION OF DONE
=======================

Upgrade-09 is complete only when:

[ ] Upgrade-08 multi-host claims are backed by real PostgreSQL tests
[ ] at least two independent workers are tested
[ ] stale fencing token is rejected
[ ] worker crash recovery is verified
[ ] DB restart behavior is verified
[ ] production container runs non-root
[ ] clean signal handling works
[ ] Compose stack boots
[ ] Kubernetes manifests/Helm chart validate
[ ] liveness/readiness are semantically correct
[ ] rolling deployment is tested
[ ] PDB/resource configuration exists where applicable
[ ] worker draining works
[ ] production API authentication exists
[ ] server-side RBAC is enforced
[ ] secrets are referenced rather than committed
[ ] OIDC/cloud deployment path avoids long-lived secrets where supported
[ ] telemetry is implemented
[ ] metrics are bounded-cardinality
[ ] actionable alerts exist
[ ] SLI/SLO definitions exist
[ ] backup is produced
[ ] backup is restored successfully
[ ] PITR is tested before claiming PITR support
[ ] DR runbook exists
[ ] at least one failure drill is automated
[ ] artifacts have SBOM
[ ] image/dependencies are scanned
[ ] immutable release identity exists
[ ] production deployment remains separately approved
[ ] no P0/P1 production-security defect remains
[ ] release gate uses real evidence

